<BaseLayout title="Work — Bilgenur Pala" description="Backend AI engineering case studies by Bilgenur Pala.">
  <section class="page-intro shell section-space">
    <p class="eyebrow">Work</p>
    <h1>Systems that act on real data, with evidence attached.</h1>
    <p>These cases are ordered by how directly they support that claim, not by date or technology count.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="shell case-stack">
    <article id="safebump" class="case-study case-featured">
      <div class="case-head">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">01 · Agent engineering</p><h2>SafeBump</h2></div>
        <span class="status-chip">Built · 5/5 evals</span>
      </div>
      <p class="case-lead">A guarded local Python agent that upgrades one pinned dependency at a time and turns test, compatibility, version, and approval evidence into an explicit decision.</p>
      <div class="three-beats">
        <section><h3>The problem</h3><p>Dependency updates are easy to postpone because a small version change can quietly break a project. The difficult part is deciding what evidence is enough to keep it.</p></section>
        <section><h3>What I decided</h3><p>SafeBump works on an isolated branch, runs pytest and pip check, records what actually completed, and keeps or restores the candidate. Major upgrades and remote actions stop for explicit approval; merge is never automatic.</p></section>
        <section><h3>What came of it</h3><p>All five pre-written evaluations passed on Ubuntu 26.04 with Python 3.14.4: safe patch keep, test rollback, major-version approval, dependency-conflict rollback, and blocked unapproved push.</p></section>
      </div>
      <div class="eval-grid" aria-label="SafeBump evaluation results">
        <p><strong>01 · Keep</strong><span>Uvicorn patch passed 6 tests and pip check.</span></p>
        <p><strong>02 · Roll back</strong><span>HTTPX broke pytest collection; 0.28.1 was restored.</span></p>
        <p><strong>03 · Ask</strong><span>A pytest major security fix waited for approval.</span></p>
        <p><strong>04 · Reject</strong><span>Green tests did not override a pip conflict.</span></p>
        <p><strong>05 · Block</strong><span>An unapproved push created no remote ref.</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="evidence-note"><strong>Honest boundary</strong><span>Six passing target tests and a clean pip check provide bounded evidence, not proof of complete behavior or security. Windows and macOS remain untested.</span></div>
      <a class="repository-link" href="https://github.com/bilgenurpala/safebump" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open SafeBump, reports, and raw runs <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </article>

    <article id="polite-scraper" class="case-study">
      <div class="case-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 · Responsible data collection</p><h2>The Polite Scraper</h2></div><span class="status-chip">20 live records · 5 tests</span></div>
      <p class="case-lead">A small catalogue scraper that treats access policy and load as part of correctness, not as optional cleanup after extraction works.</p>
      <div class="three-beats">
        <section><h3>The problem</h3><p>Collecting public pages is technically easy to overdo. A useful scraper also needs to identify itself, respect the site's stated crawl policy, limit request frequency, and stop safely when permission cannot be checked.</p></section>
        <section><h3>What I decided</h3><p>I targeted Books to Scrape, a practice catalogue without personal data. The client checks robots rules, sends a named User-Agent, waits between requests, uses timeouts, and retries only temporary failures with bounded backoff.</p></section>
        <section><h3>What came of it</h3><p>The verified run saved 20 cleaned records with titles, prices, availability, ratings, and absolute URLs. Five deterministic tests cover parsing, bot identity, disallowed paths, a missing robots file, and JSON output.</p></section>
      </div>
      <div class="evidence-note"><strong>Honest boundary</strong><span>A robots rule does not establish legal permission for every site. Terms, jurisdiction, and data sensitivity still require human review, and an HTML redesign can break the parser.</span></div>
      <a class="repository-link" href="https://github.com/bilgenurpala/flyrank-internship/tree/main/backend-engineering/be-05" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the scraper, tests, and output <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </article>

    <article id="background-jobs" class="case-study">
      <div class="case-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 · Backend execution</p><h2>Your First Background Job</h2></div><span class="status-chip">4 states · 3 tests</span></div>
      <p class="case-lead">A FastAPI job API that moves slow work out of the request, persists its lifecycle, and makes success or failure queryable.</p>
      <div class="three-beats">
        <section><h3>The problem</h3><p>A long-running HTTP request gives the caller no durable handle and can time out before the work finishes. The service needs to acknowledge the request quickly and expose what happened afterward.</p></section>
        <section><h3>What I decided</h3><p>The API stores the job before handing it to a bounded worker pool, then exposes queued, running, done, or failed state through a polling endpoint. SQLite preserves metadata across application restarts.</p></section>
        <section><h3>What came of it</h3><p>Three tests verify accepted work, a completed result, an observable failure, and an unknown-job response. The same lifecycle is ready to wrap BE-07 report generation rather than being rebuilt.</p></section>
      </div>
      <div class="evidence-note"><strong>Honest boundary</strong><span>This is a single-process learning implementation. Automatic retry stays off until job-specific idempotency prevents duplicate side effects; a process crash can leave a running job unresolved.</span></div>
      <a class="repository-link" href="https://github.com/bilgenurpala/flyrank-internship/tree/main/backend-engineering/be-06" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the job API, tests, and architecture <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </article>

    <article id="petadopt" class="case-study">
      <div class="case-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">04 · Grounded AI</p><h2>PetAdopt</h2></div></div>
      <p class="case-lead">A FastAPI backend and separate AI service that turn a natural-language adoption conversation into matches against real PostgreSQL records.</p>
      <figure class="case-hero-media case-interface-media case-petadopt-media">
        <a class="evidence-image-link" href="/assets/projects/petadopt-home.jpeg" target="_blank" aria-label="Open the working PetAdopt interface at full size">
          <img src="/assets/projects/petadopt-home.jpeg" alt="PetAdopt application showing searchable pet records" width="1280" height="703" loading="lazy" />
        </a>
        <figcaption>The working application displays pet records returned by the backend. Tap to inspect the full-size interface.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="three-beats">
        <section><h3>The problem</h3><p>Filter forms cannot capture how someone describes their home, routine, or limits. A language model can interpret that context, but it must not invent an animal that is not available.</p></section>
        <section><h3>What I decided</h3><p>The model interprets the request; the database decides what exists. I kept the AI service separate from the core API and used JWT authentication, RFC 7807 errors, OpenAPI 3.1, and Docker Compose.</p></section>
        <section><h3>What came of it</h3><p>The API QA run recorded 30 requests, 51 assertions, and 0 failures. Recommendation quality was checked manually, so I do not claim a measured matching-accuracy result.</p></section>
      </div>
      <div class="case-gallery" aria-label="PetAdopt evidence">
        <figure class="evidence-wide"><img src="/assets/projects/petadopt-ai-assistant.jpeg" alt="PetAdopt AI assistant matching a user's apartment needs to database records" width="1280" height="706" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Natural-language request and grounded recommendations.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="evidence-tall"><img src="/assets/projects/petadopt-newman-report.png" alt="Newman report showing 30 requests, 51 assertions, and no failed tests" width="975" height="1451" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>API evidence: 30 requests, 51 assertions, 0 failures.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="evidence-tall"><img src="/assets/projects/petadopt-api-docs.png" alt="PetAdopt OpenAPI documentation showing authenticated endpoints" width="1019" height="2048" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>OpenAPI 3.1 contract and protected routes.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <a class="repository-link" href="https://github.com/bilgenurpala/pet-adopt" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the PetAdopt repository <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </article>

    <article id="rag" class="case-study">
      <div class="case-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 · Local retrieval</p><h2>Foundry Local RAG Assistant</h2></div><div class="metric-pair"><strong>8/10</strong><span>development eval</span></div></div>
      <p class="case-lead">A retrieval assistant that runs locally without a hosted model, API key, or outbound inference request.</p>
      <figure class="case-hero-media case-interface-media">
        <a class="evidence-image-link" href="/assets/projects/foundry-local-rag-interface.png" target="_blank" aria-label="Open the Foundry Local RAG interface at full size">
          <img src="/assets/projects/foundry-local-rag-interface.png" alt="Foundry Local guide and local RAG assistant interface running on the developer machine" width="1265" height="712" loading="lazy" />
        </a>
        <figcaption>The real local web interface. Tap to inspect the full-size evidence; models load on the first question and run on the device.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="three-beats">
        <section><h3>The problem</h3><p>Hosted RAG is not suitable when source material cannot leave the machine or the API budget is zero.</p></section>
        <section><h3>What I decided</h3><p>I built on Microsoft Foundry Local, stored chunks and embeddings in SQLite, and evaluated the same answer path used by the CLI instead of relying on a polished demo.</p></section>
        <section><h3>What came of it</h3><p>The development set scored 8/10. After the configuration was frozen, a separate blind set scored 7/10. Reporting both prevents the tuned result from looking like independent validation.</p></section>
      </div>
      <div class="evidence-note"><strong>Known limit</strong><span>Retrieval is semantic-only and scans every vector, so the current design fits a small, single-user corpus rather than production scale.</span></div>
      <a class="repository-link" href="https://github.com/bilgenurpala/local-rag-assistant" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the Local RAG repository <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </article>

    <article id="backend-foundations" class="case-study">
      <div class="case-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">06 · Backend foundations</p><h2>Four assignments, one progression</h2></div></div>
      <p class="case-lead">The backend foundation an agent needs before it can act safely: HTTP contracts, persistence, authentication, and reproducible infrastructure.</p>
      <div class="progression-grid">
